course: religion-ethics
lesson: 37
title: Three ways of thinking: rules, consequences, character
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Thinkers have found three big ways to ask what is right: rules thinking asks "what is my duty?", consequences thinking asks "what will happen to everyone?", and character thinking asks "what would a good person do?"
explain: Rules thinking (deontology) says some things, like keeping promises and not lying, are right or wrong in themselves, even when nobody finds out.
explain: Consequences thinking (consequentialism) looks for the choice with the best results for everyone affected, but it can forget the few if it only counts the many.
explain: Character thinking (virtue ethics) says we become what we repeatedly do: practise honesty and courage and you grow honest and brave; practise greed and cruelty and you grow greedy and cruel.
explain: Often all three agree, as when you help a hurt child; when they clash, as with lying to protect someone, a strong olympiad answer looks at all three, and Christians also bring the commandments, Jesus' love and the hope of growing more like him.
fact: Rules thinking says some actions are right or wrong in themselves, whatever happens; philosophers call it deontology.
fact: Consequences thinking says the right action is the one with the best results for everyone affected; philosophers call it consequentialism.
fact: Character thinking asks what a good, kind, brave and honest person would do; philosophers call it virtue ethics.
fact: A duty is something we ought to do because it is right, like keeping a promise, even when we do not feel like it.
fact: "The greatest good for the greatest number" is a consequences idea; it can help, but it can forget the few.
fact: Virtues are good habits such as honesty and courage; vices are bad habits such as greed and cruelty.
fact: Character thinking says we become what we repeatedly do.
fact: Often all three ways point the same way, for example helping a hurt child.
fact: Sometimes rules and consequences clash, for example lying to protect someone.
fact: When the three disagree, a thinker has to weigh the reasons on each side; there is no simple formula.
fact: A strong olympiad answer often looks at rules, results and character together.
fact: Christian ethics uses all three: commandments (rules), love for neighbour's good (consequences) and growing like Christ (character).
exercise: What does rules thinking say? | Some actions are right or wrong in themselves, whatever happens | Whatever gives the best results is always the right thing to do | Do whatever a famous person would do in the same situation | There are no right or wrong actions, only feelings about them
exercise: What does consequences thinking ask? | What will happen to everyone affected by the choice | Which rule did my parents tell me to always follow at home | What would the bravest person in my whole class do here | Which choice will make me look good in front of my friends
exercise: What does character thinking ask? | What would a good, kind, honest person do here? | What does the rule book say, word for word? | Which choice gives me the most for myself? | What will most people vote for in a show of hands?
exercise: Is it wrong to break a promise even if no one finds out? Which answer uses rules thinking well? | Yes, a promise binds you because you gave your word, seen or not | No, a promise only counts if somebody is there watching you keep it | Yes, but only because you will feel bad if you get caught | No, promises are just words and words do not really matter
exercise: What are the good and bad results of telling a teacher about a cheat? Which answer weighs both? | Fairness for the class, but the cheat may be upset or angry with you | Only good results, because telling is always the right thing to do, full stop | Only bad results, because nobody likes a person who tells on others | No results at all, because teachers never do anything about cheats
exercise: Find a case where all three ways agree. | Helping a child who has fallen and hurt their knee | Telling a lie to save a friend from getting into trouble | Breaking a promise because a better party came along | Keeping quiet about a cheat because they are your friend
exercise: Which case shows rules and consequences pointing different ways? | Lying to a bully about where your friend is hiding | Saying thank you to a dinner lady for the lunch she gave you | Sharing your umbrella with a friend in the rain | Picking up litter in the park on a sunny day
exercise: What is a duty? | Something we ought to do because it is right | Something we do only when we happen to feel like it | Something we are paid money to do at work every day | Something that is always fun and easy for us to do
exercise: What duties do friends have to each other? Which is the best answer? | To be loyal and honest, and to help when the other is in trouble | To agree with everything the other one says, even when it is wrong | To lend each other money whenever the other one asks for it | To never spend time with anybody else apart from each other
exercise: What does "the greatest good" mean? | Choosing what does the most good for the most people | Choosing what is best for the person who is in charge | Choosing what the greatest king in history would choose | Choosing what feels good to you right at this moment
exercise: Is it fair to sacrifice one person's happiness for many others? Which answer is best reasoned? | Not always: many gain, but the one person still matters and has rights | Yes, always: the many win, so the one person does not count at all | No, never: what one person wants must always beat everyone else | Yes, because the one person is probably just being selfish anyway
exercise: Which is a virtue? | Courage | Greed | Cruelty | Laziness
exercise: Which is a vice? | Greed | Honesty | Patience | Kindness
exercise: What does "we become what we repeatedly do" mean? | Habits shape our character over time | We turn into whatever we eat each day | Doing a thing once fixes us for ever | Our character can never change at all
exercise: Should I tell my friend her drawing is bad? What does using all three ways suggest? | Be honest (rule), kind about it so she grows (result), as a caring friend would (character) | Say it is amazing, because lying is fine whenever it makes people happy | Say it is rubbish, because the truth is all that matters and feelings don't | Say nothing ever, because ethics cannot help with small everyday choices
exercise: Why does a strong olympiad answer look at all three ways? | It sees the problem from many angles and weighs them | It makes the answer three times as long as others | The judges will only mark answers that have three parts | One way is always right, so you need to find which one
test: My cousin says consequences thinking means "do whatever you want if it turns out fine". Put her right. | It means choosing the best results for everyone affected, not just you | Yes, if things turn out fine for you in the end, then it was right to do | Yes, and if nobody finds out then there are no results at all | It means following rules even if the results are terrible
test: Noah, who doesn't believe in God, asks: "Can I use these three ways too?" What is the best answer? | Yes, they are ways of reasoning that people of any faith or none use | No, they only work if you believe that God wrote every single one of the rules | Yes, but only the rules way, because the others need a church | No, they are secret methods that only philosophers can use
test: mate, Jack's mum asked if he was at the park and i lied to cover for him cos he'd get grounded. no harm done right? | Lying broke trust and a rule, and the "no harm" may not last; telling him to own up is wiser | Totally fine, a mate always comes first whatever happens and whatever you said | Fine, because his mum will never find out, so nothing bad happened at all | Fine, because grown-ups lie all the time so children are allowed to as well
test: A whole class gets a prize if one boy, Sam, gives up his place on the trip. A classmate says, "Easy, 29 beats 1." What is the weakness? | It counts the many but forgets Sam's fairness and his say in it | Nothing, the bigger number always wins in every kind of case like this | It is weak because 29 children could never all agree on it | It is weak because prizes are always bad for children anyway
test: Which is character thinking? | "What kind of person do I become if I keep doing this?" | "What does rule seven of the class charter say about this?" | "Which choice leads to the most points for us in the end?" | "What did the person before me decide to do in this case?"
test: Your sister says a promise doesn't count if you were in a bad mood when you made it. What would rules thinking say? | A promise freely given is still a duty, even in a bad mood | Promises in a bad mood never count, so you can ignore them | Only written promises count, spoken ones are just words | A promise only counts if keeping it is fun for you
test: A judge asks: "You said lying is always wrong. What if lying saved someone from danger?" What is the best reply? | Admit rules and results clash here, and weigh the duty against the danger | Say yes, lying is always wrong and you do not need to think about it at all | Say the judge is trying to trick you and refuse to answer the question | Say that in that case lying is always right, so there are no rules at all
test: What would happen if someone used only "the greatest good" and nothing else? | They might treat a few people unfairly to please the many | They would always be fair to every single person | They would never have to think about anyone else | They would always end up doing nothing at all
test: Which is true? | Virtues and vices both grow by being practised | Virtues come only from luck and cannot be learned | Vices disappear by themselves when we grow up | A single kind act makes a person kind for ever
test: ugh just tell me which one of the three is the RIGHT one so i can use it | Each sees something real; good answers weigh all three | Rules is always right and the other two are always wrong | Consequences is always right, rules are just for babies | Character is right only if you are already a good person
test: Holly says Christians only use rules thinking because of the Ten Commandments. What's more accurate? | Christians use rules, care for others' good, and growing like Christ | Christians are not allowed to think about results at all | Christians only care about character and never about rules | Christians never use any of these three ways of thinking
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the approach designated "deontology"? | Certain actions are right or wrong in themselves, irrespective of outcome | The rightness of an action is determined solely by its results | The rightness of an action is determined by popular approval | No action is in itself either right or wrong
exam: Identify the principal deficiency which may attend reasoning by "the greatest good of the greatest number". | It may neglect the interests of the few | It invariably neglects the majority | It forbids the weighing of consequences | It requires that no person be considered
exam: Select the response consistent with the established principles of virtue ethics. | Character is formed by the habitual repetition of actions | Character is fixed at birth and admits of no change | Character is irrelevant to the assessment of conduct | Character is acquired solely through the reading of rules
exam: Ascertain the instance wherein reasoning by rules and reasoning by consequences are liable to conflict. | The telling of a lie in order to protect another from harm | The rendering of assistance to an injured child | The expression of gratitude for a meal provided | The removal of litter from a public park
